"""Keep working in Claude Code on the Console API credit once the plan's limit is reached.

The monthly API credit of a Max plan does not cover interactive Claude Code, but
it covers `claude -p` run with an API key of the linked Console organization
(billed as Agent SDK usage); a plain pay-as-you-go API account pays for the same
calls. This script keeps one `claude -p` open for the whole run and streams the
messages to it (`--input-format stream-json`, the Agent SDK's own mode): it
starts or continues a session with its hooks, skills and CLAUDE.md, and hands it
back to interactive Claude Code afterwards with `claude --resume <id>`.

The run skips every permission prompt, so it loads the Bash firewall that sits
next to the script (`hooks/bash-firewall.py`, with `hooks/hostio.py`) for its own
run only, through `claude --settings`, and refuses to start without it. The
user's own settings are left as they are.

Usage, from the project directory:
    claude-credit               start a new session
    claude-credit -c            continue the most recent session of this directory
    claude-credit -r            list this directory's sessions and pick one
    claude-credit -r <id>       continue that session (--resume <id> works too)
    claude-credit --budget 5    with any of the above: stop taking messages once this run cost 5 $

-c and -r <id> ask before reloading a session above 300'000 tokens of context.

A message sent while Claude works is passed on at once: Claude reads it as soon
as the tool calls it is running finish, within the same turn, as in Claude Code.
Esc or Ctrl+C interrupts the turn without ending the session; queued messages go
next. `exit`, Ctrl+D on an empty input or Ctrl+C twice on an empty input ends the
run. The plain display reads lines, not keys: there Ctrl+C interrupts a turn and,
while Claude is idle, ends the run at once.

Display: with python3-prompt-toolkit and python3-rich on a terminal, the
transcript goes to the terminal's own scrollback (answers as rendered Markdown,
the user's messages behind an orange bar, one line per action) above a live
area, an input box and a status line; `/` lists the session's commands (Enter
takes the first one, as in Claude Code), Up and Ctrl+R recall the messages
typed in this directory, Ctrl+O prints the last action's full output. Without
them, when a terminal is missing or with TERM=dumb, the plain display prints
the answers and one line per tool call and reads one message per line (lines
arriving together, a paste or a pipe, wait for an empty line or the end of
input). Both end each turn with its cost and the
context in use, end more than 30 s of work with a desktop notification
(notify-send, when installed) and, on a terminal other than TERM=dumb, show ✻
in the window title while Claude works, ✓ once it is done.

Stdlib only, plus python3-rich and python3-prompt-toolkit when they are
installed. The API key is pasted at launch and never enters the shell, so
interactive sessions keep using the subscription. It does reach the commands
Claude runs during the session: Claude Code only scrubs it from subprocesses in
a mode that is incompatible with bypass permissions.

So on Linux (5.3 and later) nothing the session started outlives the run: the
script forks at once, and its first process, the guardian, waits above the run
as a child subreaper, so that orphans come back to it rather than to init.
However the run ends (exit, an error, a signal, kill -9), the guardian then ends
every process still below it (claude, a command left in the background, an
orphan of `cmd &`; not the children it had before `exec claude-credit`):
SIGTERM with SIGCONT, then SIGKILL 5 s later, saying so when it has to wait. It
names them on stderr, sets the terminal back to the mode it found, and exits
with the run's code (128 + N after signal N). Any signal another process sends
it that would end it is passed on to the run, and so is a hangup; the
keyboard's already reach both. Job control is not passed on: kill -STOP or
-TSTP to the guardian alone stops only the guardian, while the run goes on (stop
the process group, as Ctrl+Z does). During the run nothing changes: what a
crashed claude left runs until the run ends. Limits: kill -9 of the guardian
itself closes the run as a hangup would (claude ends) but sweeps nothing, and
`pkill -9 -f claude-credit` kills both; a process handed to a service outside
the tree (systemd-run, at, a tmux or screen server already running) is not
below it; another user's process (sudo) cannot be signalled, and is named at
once; one stuck in an uninterruptible system call is named after 10 s.
Elsewhere, or without /proc, the run is unguarded, as before.
"""

